<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1213">1213</a>–<a href="/years/1272">1272</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-burgundy">Capetian House of Burgundy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugh IV was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 1218 to 1272. He acquired the county of Chalon in 1237, extending the duchy, took part in the Barons' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1239 and joined Louis IX's crusade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1248–1250. His long reign was a time of prosperity and growing ducal authority in Burgundy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy" width="313" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernest Petit(1835-1918) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hugh_IV,_Duke_of_Burgundy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hugh was born on 9 March 1213, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-iii-duke-of-burgundy">Odo III, Duke of Burgundy</a>, and Alice de Vergy. His father died in 1218 during the War of the Succession of Champagne, and Hugh succeeded him as a child. As a young duke he opposed the regency of Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and joined the rebel barons in 1229, which brought him into conflict with Theobald IV, Count of Champagne. In 1237, through an exchange with John l'Antique de Chalon, he gave up the barony of Salon for the counties of Chalon and Auxonne, enlarging the duchy as the wine trade grew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On the Barons' Crusade of 1239, led by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-navarre">Theobald I of Navarre</a>, Hugh was among the nobles who wanted to fight against the advice of the king and the military orders. He withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> with Walter IV, Count of Brienne, and so avoided the defeat at Gaza. He then joined Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, helped rebuild Ascalon and took part in the peace made with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1241.</p><p>Hugh reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> by May 1249 to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a>. During the siege of Mansurah he led one of the two divisions of the army that crossed, the other being led by the king, and he was taken prisoner with Louis in the retreat at Fariskur.</p><p>In 1266 Baldwin II, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperor, promised him the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in return for help in reconquering lands lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, so that Hugh became titular King of Thessalonica. The campaign never took place, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-x">Pope Gregory X</a> held off any attempt while Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> promised a union of the churches. Hugh joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a> in 1270 but returned home after Louis IX died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hugh married first Yolande of Dreux and later Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Among his children were Robert II, who succeeded him in the duchy, and Isabella, who married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf I of Germany</a>. He died on 27 October 1272 at Villaines-en-Duismois, and his burial place is unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hugh IV of Burgundy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…III commanded the French contingent of the Third Crusade after Philip II returned home in 1191 and died at Acre in 1192, Odo III fought for Philip II at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214, and <b>Hugh IV</b> joined the crusade of Louis IX to Egypt in 1248–1250.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Villaines-en-Duesmois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Villaines-en-Duesmois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.68,4.53&amp;year=1251" aria-label="Villaines-en-Duesmois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5260 6036h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5260 6036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.68,4.53&amp;year=1251">What was here in 1251? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
